From: local
Subject: econet-eth: don't hold port->reg_lock (spinlock) across en75_qdma_use (mutex)

Confirmed by CONFIG_PROVE_LOCKING (lockdep) on real EN751627 hardware:

  [ BUG: Invalid wait context ]
  netifd/2091 is trying to lock:
  (&qdma->lock){....}-{3:3}, at: en75_qdma_use+0x1c/0xe8 [econet_eth]
  context-{4:4}
  2 locks held by netifd/2091:
   #0: (rtnl_mutex){+.+.}-{3:3}, at: dev_ioctl+0x2cc/0x5f0
   #1: (&port->reg_lock){+.+.}-{2:2}, at: en75_qdma_new+0xe90/0x20bc [econet_eth]

en75_dev_open() takes port->reg_lock (a spinlock) via guard(spinlock), whose
scope extends to the end of the function per its C cleanup-attribute
semantics -- and en75_qdma_use() (still called within that scope, at the
function's `return` statement) takes qdma->lock via mutex_lock_nested().
Taking a sleeping-capable lock (mutex) while holding a non-sleeping one
(spinlock) is what lockdep's wait-context check exists to catch.

Every other guard(spinlock)(&port->reg_lock) call site in this file (in
en75_set_gdm_port_fwd_cfg and en75_dev_change_mtu) has nothing after it
that can sleep, so this appears to be the only site affected. The intended
scope was almost certainly just the three register accesses immediately
following the lock -- en75_set_macaddr() elsewhere in this same file
already uses scoped_guard(spinlock, ...) { ... } for exactly this reason.
Narrowing en75_dev_open's spinlock to a scoped_guard around just those
register accesses (matching that existing style) drops the lock before
en75_qdma_use() is reached, resolving the invalid wait context with no
change in what's actually protected.

This function (en75_qdma_new, which en75_dev_open's call path runs
through) is also the exact function present in every stack trace of a
separate, severe symptom: en75_qdma_xmit() permanently deadlocking under
periodic multicast transmit load (see upstream bug report for the full
writeup). Not yet confirmed whether this is the same root cause manifesting
at a different call site, or a related-but-distinct locking issue in the
same function -- testing against real hardware to see whether this specific
fix also resolves the periodic deadlock, not just the boot-time lockdep hit.

--- a/econet_port.c
+++ b/econet_port.c
@@ -153,11 +153,12 @@ static int en75_dev_open(struct net_device *dev)
 	// 	airoha_fe_clear(qdma->eth, REG_GDM_INGRESS_CFG(port->id),
 	// 			GDM_STAG_EN_MASK);

-	guard(spinlock)(&port->reg_lock);
-	rlt = en75_rreg(&port->regs->rx_len_threshold);
-	set_gdm_len_th_runt_len(&rlt, 60);
-	set_gdm_len_th_oversize_len(&rlt, ETH_HLEN + dev->mtu + ETH_FCS_LEN);
-	en75_wreg(rlt, &port->regs->rx_len_threshold);
+	scoped_guard(spinlock, &port->reg_lock) {
+		rlt = en75_rreg(&port->regs->rx_len_threshold);
+		set_gdm_len_th_runt_len(&rlt, 60);
+		set_gdm_len_th_oversize_len(&rlt, ETH_HLEN + dev->mtu + ETH_FCS_LEN);
+		en75_wreg(rlt, &port->regs->rx_len_threshold);
+	}

 	return en75_qdma_use(port->qdma);
 }
